# Lesson 4, batch 2: the ruffing finesse. Dummy's ♣AQJ opposite a singleton; cash the ace, lead the queen, and throw a loser when East plays low.
title: A ruffing finesse
id: L04-22
type: exercise
lesson: 4
concepts: 4.8
student: S
board: 98
dealer: E
vulnerable: NS

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  763    7652   852    AQJ
  E  JT4    T8     T73    K9764
  S  A852   AKQJ4  A94    3
  W  KQ9    93     KQJ6   T852

auction:
  E Pass
  S 1H     = 13+ points, at least four hearts
     not *: 18 points and five hearts: open your long suit at the one level.
  W Pass
  N 2H     = 6-9 points, 3+ hearts
  E Pass
  S 4H     = Game
     not *: You have 20 points counting your singleton, and partner has at least 6: enough for game.
  W Pass
  N Pass
  E Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1♥ and partner raised to 2♥. With 20 points counting
  distribution, you bid game. You are declarer, because you bid hearts
  first, and so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  trumps out: 4
  losers: 4
  spades: 2 = The ace takes care of one spade, and dummy can ruff your fourth. That leaves two.
  hearts: 0 = Solid.
  diamonds: 2 = Your ace takes care of one diamond; the other two are losers.
  clubs: 0 = Dummy's ace takes care of your singleton.
  remind: You can afford three losers in 4♥. Count the obvious losers suit by suit in your own hand: spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs.
  remind: Dummy has the ace, queen and jack of clubs, and you have one club. How could the queen help you?
play:
  1   W DK  N D2  E D3  S D4*
      note: Four losers, one too many. Dummy's clubs can help: after the ace, lead the queen. If East covers with the king, ruff it, and dummy's jack is a winner. If East plays low, throw a loser on the queen. Either way, a loser goes, as long as East has the king.
      D4 key: Duck. You have to lose two diamonds anyway, so lose one now and keep your ace to stop the suit.
      D4 not DA: Did you think about ducking? You'll lose two diamonds either way. Losing one now keeps your ace for later.
  2   W DQ  N D5  E D7  S DA
      note: Now win the ace.
  3   S HA  W H3  N H2  E H8
      note: Draw trumps.
  4   S HK  W H9  N H5  E HT
      note: Both opponents followed twice, so trumps are drawn.
  5   S C3  W C2  N CA  E C4
      note: Over to dummy's ♣A.
  6   N CQ  E C6  S D9*  W C5
      note: Lead the ♣Q.
      D9 key: East played low, so throw your losing diamond. If East has the king, the queen wins.
      D9 not *: Did you think about throwing a loser? If East has the king, the queen wins, and you've thrown a loser for nothing. If West has it, you've only swapped one loser for another.
  7   N CJ  E CK  S H4  W C8
      note: The queen won. Now the jack: East covers with the king, and you ruff.
  8   S SA  W S9  N S3  E S4
      note: Now give up your two spade losers.
  9   S S2  W SQ  N S6  E ST
  10  W SK  N S7  E SJ  S S5
  11  W DJ  N D8  E DT  S HJ
      note: The rest are yours.
  12  S HQ  W D6  N H6  E C7
  13  S S8  W CT  N H7  E C9
      note: 10 tricks: 4♥ made.

play recap:
  The plan: you counted four losers, one too many, and dummy's clubs offered
  a way to throw one away.

  This is a ruffing finesse. You led the queen toward your hand, where you
  had no clubs left. If East had covered with the king, you would have
  ruffed it, and dummy's jack would have taken your diamond loser instead.
  East played low, so you threw the diamond at once. It works whenever East
  has the king. If West had held it, the queen would have lost, and you
  would have lost four tricks: down one.
